# Starforge Forms — modular ownership

Any Starforge module that needs user-facing forms **must** use `product:forms` — presets, definitions, visual builder, submissions, and mail hooks. Do not ship a parallel field builder, schema editor, or SMTP form UI inside the module.

## Source of truth

| Concern | Owner |
| --- | --- |
| Field schema & labels | **Forms admin** — definition id + preset under the module’s **category** (Events → Events, Support → ticket preset, etc.) |
| Seed / stable id | Module helper (e.g. `ensureEventRegistrationFormSeeded`) + preset in `src/core/forms/presets.ts` |
| Render-time context | Owning module — hidden ids, dynamic select options, terms checkbox, capacity labels |
| Placement on site | Layouts **Form** particle → definition id |

Built-in definition ids (`contact`, `event-registration`, …) are **protected** — not deletable from Forms admin.

## Do

- Register presets with `category` in `presets.ts`; group Forms admin list/create by category.
- Link from module settings to **Forms → [definition]** (“Edit registration form”) and state that Forms owns fields.
- Inject only **runtime** fields/values in the module loader (tiers, `event_id`, legal checkbox) — keep the base schema editable in Forms.
- Reuse `isProtectedFormDefinitionId`, `listFormPresetsByCategory`, and shared form actions/store APIs.

## Don’t

- Duplicate preset field arrays in module admin UIs.
- Hard-delete or fork built-in definitions modules depend on.
- Build module-specific “form designer” screens — use `/admin/forms/[id]` builder.
- Build a second canvas for form rows. The Form builder is the layout canvas with a field palette and layout-only chrome removed (`sf-ui-design.mdc`).
- Call `getMailer()` from a new module. Forms success mail is Email Manager `emitEmailTrigger` only — never fall back to the Mailer.
- Move `src/core/forms` out of Core as part of an unrelated change. The engine stays there until a dedicated extract. Presets and the admin stay this module’s job.

## Quick check

If a module collects structured user input on the public site, the schema should be editable under Forms in the right category; the module only wires context and submission handling.
